          "items": [
            [
              "Indeterminate form",
              "The limiting behavior of the pieces alone does not fix the result."
            ],
            [
              "Derivative quotient",
              "The ratio $f^\\prime/g^\\prime$ with numerator and denominator differentiated separately."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "md": {
            "file": "learn.md"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "formulas",
          "title": "Formulas to know",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "Quotient limit",
              "latex": "\\lim f/g=\\lim f^\\prime/g^\\prime",
              "note": "Only after checking the theorem’s conditions."
            },
            {
              "label": "Power transformation",
              "latex": "y=f(x)^{g(x)}>0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\ln y=g(x)\\ln f(x)",
              "note": "Return to y by exponentiating."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "warn",
          "title": "Common errors",
          "md": "Do not use the quotient rule here. Recheck before applying L’Hôpital again. For a power form, exponentiate the final log limit."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "exam",
          "title": "Written exam practice",
          "md": "Label the original form and show the rewrite. For an invalid application, explain the nonzero numerator or the missing quotient form."
        }
      ]
    },
